<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cardwell reforms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cardwell reforms were a series of changes to the British Army made by Edward Cardwell, secretary of state for war in Gladstone's first government, between 1868 and 1874. They abolished the purchase of officers' commissions and flogging in peacetime, introduced shorter enlistment with a reserve, and linked infantry battalions to home districts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cardwell-reforms.jpg" alt="Cardwell reforms" width="320" height="527" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlo Pellegrini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Cardwell,_Vanity_Fair,_1869-04-03,crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Five royal commissions dealt with army reform before 1870. The most controversial, in 1857, heard Sir Charles Trevelyan campaign against the purchase of commissions, when a captaincy cost £2,400 and promotion to lieutenant colonel £7,000; selection by merit and education was found only in the artillery and engineers. An army of only 25,000 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> had stripped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> of almost every trained soldier, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Mutiny</a> again required almost the whole usable army. Reform was resisted by senior officers led by the commander-in-chief, Prince George, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and the German victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-empire">Second French Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> gave it new urgency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1868 Cardwell abolished flogging in peacetime, though it remained on active service until 1881, and in 1869 he began withdrawing troops from the self-governing colonies, which were encouraged to raise their own forces; by 1871 some 26,000 had returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The Army Enlistment Act 1870 introduced short service: men enlisted for twelve years, served six with the colours in the infantry, eight in the line cavalry and artillery, and passed the rest in the reserve, paid fourpence a day. The War Office Act 1870 reformed the administration of the War Office.</p><p>The Regulation of the Forces Act 1871 divided the country into 66 brigade districts based on county boundaries and population. Each line infantry regiment was to have two battalions sharing a depot and recruiting area, one serving overseas while the other trained at home, with the local militia usually as a third; the linking of regiments was completed only under the Childers Reforms of 1881. When a bill reimbursing officers for their commissions was defeated, the government announced that all purchase was abolished, and the House of Lords then passed the remedial legislation. Second lieutenant replaced the ranks of cornet and ensign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A change of government removed Cardwell in 1874, but his reforms survived attempts to undo them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Garnet Wolseley</a>, his principal military adviser from 1871, went on defending short service, and by 1900 the reserve numbered about 80,000 trained men; in 1899 it allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to assemble for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> the largest army it had ever deployed abroad. Historians of the army have generally praised the reforms as essential to modernisation, while a minority, among them Theodore Hoppen, call them partial at best, since no planning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> or chief of staff was created.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cardwell Reforms” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army, small and long-service, was mainly employed in imperial wars in India, Afghanistan, southern Africa and elsewhere, and, alongside France and the Ottoman Empire, in the Crimean War (1853–1856), whose mismanagement prompted reform; the <b>Cardwell reforms</b> of 1868–1874 abolished the purchase of officers’ commissions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Military</a></li></ul></section>
